/ref 17592186398906} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5e99be-19eb-595d-af93-4a58dae57124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7537fda6-1347-5a5e-82ca-61565175e063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398904} :answer-field/id #uuid "c150e887-e652-5f7f-bc4d-da8fa5b5c6c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38244b69-b2ff-532d-ba91-ffcf585c5c43" :question/math-academy-id "q-50202" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$, then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int ({e}^{t}-4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e4267cc-bd1e-507b-8072-2b2a8c2eca09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330502} {:answer/id #uuid "8b417cb4-a6eb-5024-a1dc-ec0c00436d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331187} {:answer/id #uuid "a75d6c04-3e75-57b3-9835-3be982051f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331442} {:answer/id #uuid "bea71f13-c9fb-5f86-9a53-ebbcbb8c38fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331639} {:answer/id #uuid "ee34aca2-9235-5324-8e51-2b3c711ec180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bea71f13-c9fb-5f86-9a53-ebbcbb8c38fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331639} :answer-field/id #uuid "d34e31ac-faf7-57f2-94a5-145454205303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35b7f010-fdff-57b3-be05-b9c6b80db56c" :question/math-academy-id "q-50220" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}=0$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{x+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{1+C} \\\\ 1+C & =1 \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}=0$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e989ff47-d410-5b01-a943-55fae4916d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392717} {:answer/id #uuid "d2815ebf-4757-56c9-91cd-cb0eb91dbf32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392718} {:answer/id #uuid "41cb0d63-902e-524a-bf02-6e3c4af5fca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392719} {:answer/id #uuid "92c3b147-4088-52d5-b2f3-b4ece5e73d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392720} {:answer/id #uuid "e77630a7-5ba0-527b-b582-20214c8e5890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e989ff47-d410-5b01-a943-55fae4916d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392717} :answer-field/id #uuid "b56c8992-3610-5b4f-9996-cd46ce9731ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f83185ca-7204-529d-8697-1b2d513b1e26" :question/math-academy-id "q-50222" :question/problem "$\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aedc002-f5a6-54e6-9744-7dcd1264c9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{5t-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330044} {:answer/id #uuid "94c7559f-dc37-58c5-8f05-31b0f239cbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2+5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331267} {:answer/id #uuid "9770e4f7-8ffd-5efd-b347-5be76f613443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331298} {:answer/id #uuid "99afcf09-a4ab-59a9-a911-b1d8a5327735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{2-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331327} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b97d74-536f-52a5-baba-826d10b2969c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{5-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9770e4f7-8ffd-5efd-b347-5be76f613443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331298} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dff75f0-f423-53f7-90ad-5ea82c6cdb2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "730becc0-14c6-5133-bbd8-aaf700159913" :question/math-academy-id "q-50242" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=2{e}^{3t},\\,y=3{e}^{t+2}-5$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{e}^{t+2}}{6{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t})}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{2-2t}}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "072ad553-dd0b-58e0-b3e2-082c6d9866dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fde686e-0040-50c8-9b9b-1ad4f1e8aaa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392724} {:answer/id #uuid "d02ac812-19ad-5839-9ad9-16c2a59beade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392725} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4ffd3e-cd2a-5031-83aa-72de86237fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392726} {:answer/id #uuid "dd140ea3-6577-5157-ab09-4fefef73b8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2{e}^{2t}-{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392727} {:answer/id #uuid "37a5973e-637b-5d29-9329-f2c326b69242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d02ac812-19ad-5839-9ad9-16c2a59beade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392725} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ef94ed-b793-5302-a468-c42ecf1df591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "607151f7-a0f9-522f-b6fe-540456f7af54" :question/math-academy-id "q-50371" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}+1},\\,{t}^{5}⟩,$ ${r}^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}+1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{{e}^{t}({e}^{t}+1)-{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)={t}^{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨{x}^{′}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5{t}^{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e04567-d2e1-5eef-aee3-62d784a9fe49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve Given the First Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f191b13-825b-5416-8838-96dcdfe09985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330218} {:answer/id #uuid "9872acd7-7eb7-50ae-9b3c-851760c20bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(1-2t)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331311} {:answer/id #uuid "99ac4658-4973-5057-bf1a-ac90579a65ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331325} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4c8a27-8293-5412-8e68-c58c6bc8b6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(2t-1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331741} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2c6554-4cb6-5fba-be9f-c05762bf19fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-4t}(t+1)}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f191b13-825b-5416-8838-96dcdfe09985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330218} :answer-field/id #uuid "209fa9dc-670a-542f-8478-5c1255d77e7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daa33906-2d77-5e9e-b519-e39e6466e412" :question/math-academy-id "q-50448" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-1,\\,y={e}^{-4t}.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}-1)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8t{e}^{-4t}+2{e}^{-4t}}{{t}^{2}})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8t{e}^{-4t}+2{e}^{-4t}}{2{t}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0e2596-73d6-540c-af84-b19a943da6a8" :knowledge-point/title "The Second Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe19103f-5f99-576d-a025-053904208525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392731} {:answer/id #uuid "e01882fe-f747-5560-b961-3aeaa74d08c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,{sec}^{3}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392732} {:answer/id #uuid "574da43a-a87f-5e11-8117-2e74261fac00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392733} {:answer/id #uuid "8eccfb9d-3d94-5276-98aa-105bb31f0184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392734} {:answer/id #uuid "13350f1d-caa5-59e1-8249-6fc0fdc305b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,{sec}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "574da43a-a87f-5e11-8117-2e74261fac00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392733} :answer-field/id #uuid "30603a02-99ff-59c6-861c-ce870828f5cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fd0a18c-43f2-5761-ad4b-96e6a87257fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-50467" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩,$ ${r}^{″}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{3}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae63895f-26a5-550a-9e82-7414623e5158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063385} {:answer/id #uuid "df6a43b6-1b71-5020-aa36-4c0f562445ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → - 1^{-})f(x) \\ne lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063386} {:answer/id #uuid "19140e5f-24a2-5763-ba7e-b7d994f1db7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → - 1)f(x) \\ne f(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063387} {:answer/id #uuid "2403be5a-750d-57cf-99a8-51653ca8fe77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063388} {:answer/id #uuid "32cdefd9-4378-561b-a0cd-f3b8e222c2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063389}] :answer-field/id #uuid "07a26449-9faf-5a21-a528-5c30e4b24664" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063384} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4b4b7f1-de87-51e2-9ed4-2f2cacacaf29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403019} {:answer/id #uuid "84f08e7a-96c7-57e5-8318-9498325cc5c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403020} {:answer/id #uuid "de5ec02c-27a2-5d7d-8fe3-b0875d4201ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403021} {:answer/id #uuid "60166612-0904-52f0-96ae-69b9e3a2f0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403022} {:answer/id #uuid "3265516b-d79e-586e-a42e-0a19b7a4fb6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)\\ne f(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84f08e7a-96c7-57e5-8318-9498325cc5c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403020} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9ecb96-b0c8-577b-ba89-04233ae88d06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca98ec7d-0763-558c-b634-f1da93c05079" :question/math-academy-id "q-51054" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e68ed1-6c21-5339-b817-0dca6bed6e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330010} {:answer/id #uuid "13dd78f4-7836-5e2c-95cf-18fff06f843d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330114} {:answer/id #uuid "36a4f226-546a-58fe-9e0a-5f6172d50d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330439} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbffc66-920f-5159-966d-348e80294b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331207} {:answer/id #uuid "fea5d391-e5d1-5006-b2d7-39671ae5c2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fea5d391-e5d1-5006-b2d7-39671ae5c2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332194} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ab9f03-8ca5-52dc-a6f7-f2d81ee5a0b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "227ca0fe-b4ce-570a-82ea-2de7f8f95c49" :question/math-academy-id "q-51067" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$, has a cusp at $x=1,$ and is differentiable at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=-2$\n- It's **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x+2|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a cusp at $x=1$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4edaf09a-43ea-57b9-acbf-f37078f4f41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330665} {:answer/id #uuid "940c2245-8eff-5f28-9ff9-b6f020a49afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331263} {:answer/id #uuid "de0ae6a9-dc8c-5d6d-81ca-a6a089d7492b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331913} {:answer/id #uuid "ed63afa6-ed56-546d-b92e-5cd7efd76af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332036} {:answer/id #uuid "fe84f7ce-c9f5-5fd2-a3fe-29ac648392b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "940c2245-8eff-5f28-9ff9-b6f020a49afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331263} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcf8469a-015f-5427-bd41-e940a4f01f94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ce07619-7707-5d93-86b3-865c0def2d71" :question/math-academy-id "q-51072" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x+4, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ x+2, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(-2,2)$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false and statement III is true. Indeed,  let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(-x+4)=-1+4=3 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(x+2)=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it has a removable discontinuity there). Hence, it is also not differentiable at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04669e9c-9d0f-58e8-8793-3657d9ee41f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329996} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd4d6b6-8ac8-54bc-9249-04799b6eaf49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330194} {:answer/id #uuid "43a379dd-8a5a-50c9-8642-f63a231a3698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330557} {:answer/id #uuid "64586c0d-af1a-5647-926a-a4b555ad5d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330844} {:answer/id #uuid "67546541-7bbf-543c-b06f-2d38778501d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bd4d6b6-8ac8-54bc-9249-04799b6eaf49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330194} :answer-field/id #uuid "11564464-e4af-50c7-963e-46a79dfa1f3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d136f69-0617-5d12-ad3b-b952611b8b09" :question/math-academy-id "q-51075" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({0}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to -\\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "450aebfe-d896-50bc-a392-e43aee7afabd" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Rational Function With Two Linear Factors as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66d28f82-ac5b-5ae8-ac49-3837b5b1a0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330871} {:answer/id #uuid "756a3b47-ad40-5f60-9997-64713626c9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331003} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b69123-e74a-59d5-8172-fc7b6a40e7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331550} {:answer/id #uuid "bda8cb78-3b56-5886-93d1-f64c837a2d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331626} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7e6df2-3a00-5165-a4ca-d91a0c640677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bda8cb78-3b56-5886-93d1-f64c837a2d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331626} :answer-field/id #uuid "b852ba36-bd01-51f8-af0d-af8a533d3801" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b63aac4-0a1a-5d37-898e-889fc9ac99c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-51134" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{(x-1)(x+1)}=\\frac{3}{(x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+1)},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+5}{(x-1)(x+1)}=\\frac{3}{(x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-1)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\nx+5=3(x+1)+B(x-1).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-1$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+5 & =3(-1+1)+B(-1-1) \\\\ 4 & =-2B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "450aebfe-d896-50bc-a392-e43aee7afabd" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Rational Function With Two Linear Factors as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0954e5da-40a5-5bd3-b5f9-fc1576bb42fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330030} {:answer/id #uuid "0bab2cd7-8b65-59ea-906e-cc5493b16927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330051} {:answer/id #uuid "3c25b332-997d-55d4-95bf-d488a2db06e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330484} {:answer/id #uuid "96364be9-762f-5cc3-884a-b30f146e575c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331283} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e3390d-53f7-57b7-9619-2d45def6908f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bab2cd7-8b65-59ea-906e-cc5493b16927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330051} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8885759-82af-576a-8062-56965278058a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "337c013e-15c0-5b6b-95be-a5f36745ed16" :question/math-academy-id "q-51135" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+2}{(x+2)(x-1)}=\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{2}{(x-1)},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x+2}{(x+2)(x-1)}=\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{2}{(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x+2)(x-1)$ gives\n\n$$\n4x+2=A(x-1)+2(x+2).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-2)+2 & =A(-2-1)+2(-2+2) \\\\ -6 & =-3A \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f9bea82-b38e-5841-aebd-2d90d435d3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331076} {:answer/id #uuid "a9cb0473-c108-5e1b-b9ef-fce32f58728a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331456} {:answer/id #uuid "bff3872b-4b7d-5892-a9cb-b5f660bb2a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331653} {:answer/id #uuid "df921d04-bc75-5a22-96f2-73388b9d0ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331924} {:answer/id #uuid "f34074ee-e62c-5996-abbf-063c96890ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9bea82-b38e-5841-aebd-2d90d435d3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331076} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cb3f2db-52ae-5ed2-9744-e38318f3fb12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bf4d6fe-76cd-565c-9382-083cab13afc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-51145" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x+5}{({x}^{2}+2)(x-1)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+2}+\\frac{2}{x-1},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+2)(x-1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-3x+5 & =(2x+B)(x-1)+2({x}^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}-3(0)+5 & =(2(0)+B)(0-1)+2({0}^{2}+2) \\\\ 5 & =(B)(-1)+2(2) \\\\ 5 & =-B+4 \\\\ 1 & =-B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20824866-0419-512a-b241-e3eb66b30194" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial For a Function Given Some Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f5e82b7-75dd-51c9-8a60-1834ac672dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+(x-3)+(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330512} {:answer/id #uuid "4cacd093-026c-5b5b-9638-12b541c3f156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+(x-3)+(x-3)^{2}+3(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330647} {:answer/id #uuid "599903f5-f809-5b77-a096-0359884c8f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+(x-3)+(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330752} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4f2677-b512-5373-b072-27a990eae8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+(x-3)+2(x-3)^{2}+3(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331459} {:answer/id #uuid "aceba638-d03d-59aa-a7d5-4171c77027cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+(x-3)+2(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "599903f5-f809-5b77-a096-0359884c8f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+(x-3)+(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330752} :answer-field/id #uuid "1de9e27e-5aa9-51f0-8dae-892588b60777" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "640afc35-330c-5055-be1c-03cf89541fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-51238" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function with $f(3)=1,{f}^{′}(3)=1,{f}^{″}(3)=2$ and ${f}^{‴}(3)=3.$ Which of the following is the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=3?$" :question/worked-solution "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2!}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(3)}{3!}(x-3)^{3} \\\\  & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(3)}{6}(x-3)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have been given the following set of values: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(3) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(3) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =1+1(x-3)+\\frac{2}{2}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{3}{6}(x-3)^{3} \\\\  & =1+(x-3)+(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-3)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9785058c-329b-55fc-9637-7ceee865761c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3, 1)$ and $(1, 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063397} {:answer/id #uuid "aded52a6-93b0-511f-9f1b-d35eef943b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3, 5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063398} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d58dbe-fb8b-5092-b964-5ef385a425a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063399} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9fa222-9117-541a-9cf6-4897b1674212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3, 1)$ and $(3, 5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063400} {:answer/id #uuid "57a776a1-07fd-5790-8507-49d58efecc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 3)$ and $(1, 5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063401}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2597a55f-1687-5449-83d5-f62940cb9a48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ea1609e-b20a-54a8-915b-5dda82c99744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403025} {:answer/id #uuid "e543617b-6351-5c17-a3f6-e2802c494112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403026} {:answer/id #uuid "c568cebe-030f-5cf8-b856-afd17200378c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403027} {:answer/id #uuid "3d165dca-76a6-5440-b19b-8c808cb523b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,1)$ and $(3,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403028} {:answer/id #uuid "523960b9-215b-5ec5-9e2e-830b99e6b9c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ and $(1,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ea1609e-b20a-54a8-915b-5dda82c99744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403025} :answer-field/id #uuid "56e62435-0b4a-5c11-a074-69bafa619ea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd3f3945-1d74-5b64-9e26-f19b51b6c5b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-51250" :question/problem "![](images/23/237c36808ded541ed264826866db3d2774dc2d5212f1292f21a6c25b3b07d0e1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($⌢$) over the interval $(1,3).$\n\n![](images/a7/a7d9436275984c12271f2c737eb005e1eaab5b308c2204040e7aff5e87faffb3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129116 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff3695f-4968-57f8-af00-a415f06e0483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330085} {:answer/id #uuid "13b81038-d872-5ca5-992a-2dd223a7ad16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100+P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330113} {:answer/id #uuid "1e971fa7-f202-5ff0-bb2c-8759fc4463dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100k,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330213} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2532e1-0f49-5d4a-80a7-8c06f2dc48ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330780} {:answer/id #uuid "9598e48f-e958-5852-a95c-d70fae680128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ff3695f-4968-57f8-af00-a415f06e0483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330085} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb8e76e-8612-579b-862b-b2c6caf17420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cbb7150-61e1-50bf-8ac9-84dfe58c7fcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-51251" :question/problem "The population of pines in a forest increases by $100$ trees every year. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the population $P(t)$ of the pines? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in years." :question/worked-solution "If the pines population is $P,$ then the rate of change of the population is $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in years.\n\nSince $100$ new pines are added every year, the rate of change of the population of the pines is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129119 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6840470a-70d6-5804-b3cb-88a0c12a3127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330888} {:answer/id #uuid "82a09dc0-8938-5a2b-a4e6-81bcb7a21ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331103} {:answer/id #uuid "96163e8d-e6e8-5255-883a-60386894bfe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331281} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ddb1da-5410-5e3b-a981-46b37e811d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331844} {:answer/id #uuid "e932b754-ebb2-556a-a8f7-1616e5abef31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k-\\frac{1}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5ddb1da-5410-5e3b-a981-46b37e811d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331844} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b3c1d96-319e-5645-a139-8d6ce71b1306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "463aab95-898d-5e27-959a-1acad2e1639f" :question/math-academy-id "q-51254" :question/problem "The number of bacteria in a particular crop at any given moment decreases at a rate that is proportional to the number of bacteria at that moment.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of bacteria $B$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of bacteria** in a particular crop at any given moment **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to **the number of bacteria** at that moment..* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of bacteria* in a particular crop varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of bacteria* at the moment. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}∝B.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of bacteria *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1040edda-0303-5346-ac49-2025a711486e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(1-x)ln⁡(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330092} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3385ec-93b4-59c7-a196-58be5d17d757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{(1-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330189} {:answer/id #uuid "430a86b3-46fa-5177-b8b1-9abf9afaa40a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(1-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330550} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2c3511-369b-5b02-8467-198f1bcf8279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-x)ln⁡(10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330941} {:answer/id #uuid "afdc0472-7689-57f5-b5c2-148cb66217a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡(10)}{(1-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1040edda-0303-5346-ac49-2025a711486e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(1-x)ln⁡(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330092} :answer-field/id #uuid "85a34950-0b5d-5d67-b7f8-2bcede974234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c643137-d804-5b6f-93e8-568de57f1d93" :question/math-academy-id "q-51317" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=log⁡(1-x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=log⁡u={log}_{10}⁡(u),\\,u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(10)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(10)}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)ln⁡(10)}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1-x)ln⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b727927-159a-5fe5-a64a-6115a05702ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5-10{h}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330633} {:answer/id #uuid "626b77f3-44ba-50fa-9b4f-747cba46c5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330833} {:answer/id #uuid "abef283a-96fd-5d43-9839-c721e873e525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331477} {:answer/id #uuid "de31ec1c-d18c-57c3-889f-c9f961627921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{h}^{-1}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331914} {:answer/id #uuid "eb34821c-31b6-535d-a27a-3ffd7caa245d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10{h}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abef283a-96fd-5d43-9839-c721e873e525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331477} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbb88348-b18d-5779-8ef2-642d021be174" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19c0268a-8fa3-5a09-89dc-e326b80c48a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-51346" :question/problem "If $h(x)=5-10x,$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=5-10x,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=5-10y\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(5-10y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-10 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36a5829f-8fb5-5220-9a62-df953efe17f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2{h}^{-1}(x)+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330440} {:answer/id #uuid "a8361423-3584-5605-8b3e-75e7c63bf9a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{h}^{-1}(x)+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331447} {:answer/id #uuid "aaffd22a-c17e-5b44-b412-6c5bb1e52839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2{h}^{-1}(x)+5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331465} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ae6d38-d6c6-5d1c-8616-d987f049ea55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2{h}^{-1}(x)+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331860} {:answer/id #uuid "fea2d729-c658-5748-9e5e-6ab0aa1ad612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2{h}^{-1}(x)+5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8361423-3584-5605-8b3e-75e7c63bf9a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{h}^{-1}(x)+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331447} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a22b11e-c1fe-5207-abdd-06c9964565db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf33d691-a1fd-5629-a53c-c90ffc853605" :question/math-academy-id "q-51347" :question/problem "If $h(x)=\\sqrt{2x+5},$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\sqrt{2x+5},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2y+5}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{2y+5}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}((2y+5)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2y+5)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2y+5}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt{2y+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\sqrt{2{h}^{-1}(x)+5} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\sqrt{2{h}^{-1}(x)+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096623 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1afe699c-49b0-55b2-9fb8-f60bbb46522d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330188} {:answer/id #uuid "2a30b0cd-654f-5e65-b15b-b2d600111c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330324} {:answer/id #uuid "994eeecb-d2e7-5d33-99b5-69227aa63901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331321} {:answer/id #uuid "99c00d2a-0985-5f3f-97a1-9aa61bafa71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331328} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbb0baf-70ec-55db-b1d6-7e1ea133ca50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1afe699c-49b0-55b2-9fb8-f60bbb46522d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330188} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a24031-5017-5ef4-aff5-6ed6c6d3d9e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2792f399-d353-50d3-affd-f9260fbd9716" :question/math-academy-id "q-51428" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8fadcc272d96492194b2d46f1ae66151fb4fd16b2ee42a6840ff335aa0b4262.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed: \n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=3.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=5$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=5.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e973d9a-0f09-5900-bf22-eef66722e865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403032} {:answer/id #uuid "47cac611-9920-5c71-952f-dd8cb39ccdf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403033} {:answer/id #uuid "4042983e-d1df-528a-8fe4-25f17290d359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403034} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b0f23a-5c95-5614-8f48-1c8705ee4818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403035} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e429b3-2ae0-5777-a461-216e6c6244c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3e429b3-2ae0-5777-a461-216e6c6244c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403036} :answer-field/id #uuid "38c59fe2-2779-5ba6-9c0d-69d20ff0ea27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1beed24-efd2-587e-8b4f-b044374db684" :question/math-academy-id "q-51432" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c8969fae3e21c8e5f5019509636e2307211921d742e6f067d9ee1c50499541b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upwards like a smile ($⌣$) over the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3).$\n\n![](images/ae/ae9ce6ca2597a4d5d1ca150c1e97d7c7a0a35f59fe5d6421b75203c6882d2052.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "697f9bd8-9edf-5643-8cdb-a821b6dce09c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6ffeffb-ef59-54f5-be3f-a2062b83999e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(2,-3)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077145} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e22452-4b20-5bf4-9090-9c4baa1361ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(3,-2)$, radius: $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077146} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f09b0a-5fff-5884-866a-7cd7c2b038b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(3,-2)$, radius: $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077147} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f5be55-2167-551f-a536-04e193c35eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-2, 3)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077148} {:answer/id #uuid "00169a7a-33bd-5ce6-8aa9-6f23a52c4730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(2,-3)$, radius: $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077149}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b0de6a19-461b-5d8f-8397-c1cfef2f142b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae470676-bc8c-59ed-9ddf-e6bfad4e99f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(2,-3)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396095} {:answer/id #uuid "3958fd13-48da-5731-9bd7-2332d7318f31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(2,-3)$, radius: $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396096} {:answer/id #uuid "445b916b-1ba8-5248-91b4-ef8043ebe2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-2,3)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396097} {:answer/id #uuid "1caf2e7f-b2a3-5b2b-9e2c-a85b6dd512fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(3,-2)$, radius: $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396098} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7bd3d5-18da-5a40-acfa-fa44fce357be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(3,-2)$, radius: $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "445b916b-1ba8-5248-91b4-ef8043ebe2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-2,3)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396097} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f64383e-34d9-58e2-8c5a-1486b312000b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd6c82b9-14ab-538d-aa0f-037306546c3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-5144" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-6y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-6y & =3 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x)+({y}^{2}-6y) & =3 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}) & =3+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+4)+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 3y+9) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-2))^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-2,3)$ and its radius is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100850 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f41b2bd-0c1d-5267-b91d-7aba35ea7efd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aae66fd-ae3d-5392-898b-c5a791b8fa34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330328} {:answer/id #uuid "374063ca-baab-5538-a588-e44eb9e351af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330445} {:answer/id #uuid "69d020a8-aeb6-591c-8849-ba5b3f0b8e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330898} {:answer/id #uuid "7eeaf714-ebb1-5b01-a873-d61e8b19c2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331069} {:answer/id #uuid "bd08c7c5-5339-5724-af5d-4464616046a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eeaf714-ebb1-5b01-a873-d61e8b19c2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331069} :answer-field/id #uuid "746e171d-dca7-52d5-b8e1-7827a7a7c939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d22c876e-b740-5eb1-b101-f8b00fac57fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-51443" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7aff74d99be667ea7b51bfcfaee2c0e701e3a553656aa931ba3f1f1ba0115ffb.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [0,1]$ and the piecewise linear for $x\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "697f9bd8-9edf-5643-8cdb-a821b6dce09c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da01d3e1-9120-5411-866d-68f2c61e8b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-4,-5)$, radius: $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396102} {:answer/id #uuid "7347a868-a565-5dcd-9bc6-dcb157568620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-4,-5)$, radius: $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396103} {:answer/id #uuid "4a914338-4d65-53bf-b781-37800f0d7a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-4,-5)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396104} {:answer/id #uuid "ebef622a-7402-5186-b9c9-cdb47181e259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(4,5)$, radius: $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396105} {:answer/id #uuid "dff6112c-7516-520a-ad8d-0c891d4eb56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(4,5)$, radius: $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dff6112c-7516-520a-ad8d-0c891d4eb56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(4,5)$, radius: $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396106} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7cc307e-c24b-55cb-bd12-ec807df438fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ac8461a-29d0-5698-901e-84a35dae5276" :question/math-academy-id "q-5183" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is ${y}^{2}-8x-10y+{x}^{2}=8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8x-10y+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-8x)+({y}^{2}-10y) & =8 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}) & =8+{4}^{2}+{5}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}) & =49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} & ={7}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(4,5)$ and its radius is $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e908bba-8bc6-5c10-9c84-aecd35277512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=3,\\,w=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330073} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4731c8-fa0d-53b2-bd23-87efb29ee2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=5,\\,w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330798} {:answer/id #uuid "65844d51-bacc-5d3d-a1e1-675d53338e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4,\\,w=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330855} {:answer/id #uuid "ba117ea5-28cb-50ec-9e7c-bd0a5d4c558e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-3,\\,w=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331592} {:answer/id #uuid "d39b148c-e51a-5bb7-a7b5-5480c62f13a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-3,\\,w=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba117ea5-28cb-50ec-9e7c-bd0a5d4c558e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-3,\\,w=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331592} :answer-field/id #uuid "e56dda56-b48f-5387-836b-e5ed6f439972" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4c56a55-59ed-53e3-ab00-d41b4f2401b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-52202" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}v-2w=5 \\\\ -v-\\frac{2}{5}w=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}v-2w=5 & |\\times 3 \\\\ -v-\\frac{2}{5}w=5 & |\\times 5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5v-6w=15 \\\\ -5v-2w=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $v$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{5v}-6w & =15 \\\\ -\\cancel{5v}-2w & =25 \\\\ (-6w-2w) & =(15+25) \\\\ -8w & =40 \\\\ w & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $w=-5$ into the first equation to solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5v-6w & =15 \\\\ 5v-6(-5) & =15 \\\\ 5v+30 & =15 \\\\ 5v & =-15 \\\\ v & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $v=-3,\\,w=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cd1bb61-2770-5096-83d6-d1e4a7189e9a" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing \"Or Equal To\" Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6c66b28-305f-5672-966e-0edbadb3f68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y\\ge 2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403039} {:answer/id #uuid "22d57f63-0dab-54f1-a015-47cd4fa3ad27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y\\le 2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403040} {:answer/id #uuid "c282cd0f-a1b4-575a-bd8d-6fbc31f00808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y<2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403041} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc09857-90e2-5fa6-8e10-a5896664af24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 3x+1 \\\\ y\\le 2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403042} {:answer/id #uuid "076f9837-2705-5d0a-9c57-71c316b16391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 3x+1 \\\\ y\\ge 2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6c66b28-305f-5672-966e-0edbadb3f68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y\\ge 2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403039} :answer-field/id #uuid "6391aa03-c66c-510d-8de3-714552c986fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9187138f-ae2f-5a56-952a-6135b67724bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-52413" :question/problem "![](images/75/75a7f3bfae55a97fbc0c05e2e0356d21de6ceabcc82ac3e1c965838a636f5ecc.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=3x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 3x+1.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110651 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbd52408-5f2f-58c9-9a88-bd992fe8752c" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Strict Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "599f73e4-3ca9-5922-b9a5-51c11d904876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<2x+6 \\\\ y>5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403046} {:answer/id #uuid "88ca59a7-54b0-51b6-8e85-e1665efeaa4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<2x+6 \\\\ y<5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403047} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2ec7bb-7f05-5512-a12e-01138a6573c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 2x+6 \\\\ y\\le 5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403048} {:answer/id #uuid "17af44de-3d26-5293-8028-23cd473e2e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>2x+6 \\\\ y<5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403049} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ebc338-a51c-54a2-9008-89b303d45e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>2x+6 \\\\ y>5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88ca59a7-54b0-51b6-8e85-e1665efeaa4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<2x+6 \\\\ y<5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403047} :answer-field/id #uuid "057029ec-da64-5909-af01-3dd51581c107" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d48a81ca-ca4e-5354-bd11-f73315ba7d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-52461" :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba01cf5268f2bd62835c925a5eb793c0d98663948899c9e9aba2eeacd37ff30a.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=2x+6.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<2x+6.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=5.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y<2x+6 \\\\ y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a3a43a-0340-5e3c-9bcf-b1d4cc0763a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330152} {:answer/id #uuid "4b779a67-3896-55fc-be1f-97694c6c476f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330635} {:answer/id #uuid "af563452-6627-5310-af3e-8f3e9187bdf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331496} {:answer/id #uuid "efd2938a-788b-5b82-8a39-c673b2cf6bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332058} {:answer/id #uuid "effab392-c1df-59af-aa5f-e43acece335d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x+1)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efd2938a-788b-5b82-8a39-c673b2cf6bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332058} :answer-field/id #uuid "59e8f7ca-397d-5324-b14e-634be47e4ef9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0793571e-bec3-5375-a73d-bbdb84782691" :question/math-academy-id "q-52522" :question/problem "Rewrite $2{x}^{2}-4x+3$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{3}{2}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}) & =2((x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a795cb-9f0e-5939-900e-adafa303f8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330103} {:answer/id #uuid "586c59c4-4661-5f6d-8c14-a8719591fc01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330738} {:answer/id #uuid "72076787-7552-58b8-8ad4-00b516cbaf7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330976} {:answer/id #uuid "98ee28e9-1737-5e3b-81c5-05141104f588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331315} {:answer/id #uuid "f630c36b-13b8-5df3-93c0-7e5b92d1a0cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586c59c4-4661-5f6d-8c14-a8719591fc01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330738} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d388fcd-2c84-5c86-86ee-03feb01e614c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38a732d9-8b0e-58f3-ab88-f23eff634c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-52537" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{x}^{2}-2x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x) & =6({(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}) \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ddff9c-ac54-5bdb-8e05-4c9e80b9afd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329975} {:answer/id #uuid "30d8498f-8b3e-524c-b327-f7f1af56ad01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330381} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8ac45d-48a3-5d22-9f35-e862992b052f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y+1)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330655} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e12e2d-9ac1-56f3-b293-e94794a6a02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332078} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb7f176-7c6f-5b6e-950c-ed129b00ef62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb7f176-7c6f-5b6e-950c-ed129b00ef62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332171} :answer-field/id #uuid "064cff71-f6ed-52e2-8855-063c335fc1b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4b82526-21d1-5906-b903-379b7a33781d" :question/math-academy-id "q-52544" :question/problem "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+6y+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3.$ So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{3}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}) & =-3({(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083380 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97802ae1-4976-5fdf-bbe1-bcd001d5e53a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Formula for the Sum of a Geometric Series Given the First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34fd762b-3662-551e-8b4b-f9a520fe71b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}({3}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330417} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9b44f3-f9cf-5117-81fc-6be65731c704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}({15}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330673} {:answer/id #uuid "84249151-7ca2-51de-be93-b9b58516c14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}(1-{15}^{N})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331123} {:answer/id #uuid "cb426507-613d-5d34-a36e-120d8f134da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}(1-{3}^{N})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331753} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2f0f84-728a-5a44-b828-2f135b4d52a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}({15}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc2f0f84-728a-5a44-b828-2f135b4d52a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}({15}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332166} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c93a0f9-a5ec-56fc-9ad2-3a8cbd183d3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5aa1f2d-4869-5112-9961-2ddb550ec79c" :question/math-academy-id "q-5259" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the first term is $3$ and the common ratio is $15?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=3$ and $r=15.$ Therefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{3(1-{15}^{N})}{1-15} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{14}(1-{15}^{N}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}({15}^{N}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115410 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a76efbd-cefd-5aa0-953e-8ab50bec7ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Height Corresponding to an Extended Base"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c69a7c27-09ce-5cf4-9117-95fab7c198f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d525b53f78cef4edb905e328ae77913f49860ca2dc2c6e1fac9db76873ac7d2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392744} {:answer/id #uuid "d0db377a-eee0-547b-a96b-5bf2faaeab19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/46b12df6f2c0d61cc7296921fb335337878249ff2fe16cbab87f0048c4abb538.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392745} {:answer/id #uuid "05352607-bc7f-52c9-b024-45731a62e3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8ed829c8e900ee2849d49202daedfe5c0978e5d6991fcce4bfe6c609aca3fb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392746} {:answer/id #uuid "9842c750-4db7-5d95-8ed5-7083875ea3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae60cff8e3670c2462ca1cf67179f6534f091c2f8bbbe4e1684141d78516e138.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392747} {:answer/id #uuid "38a9fbb8-74f8-5733-8270-a1e4e8bc058a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df918f525274479ddf12fd1940408be116c92dbe93ca19e46676ec07dc5121a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218639